+++
title: Miletus
description: Ancient Greek city in Asia-Minor
hook: After Persia crushed the Ionian Revolt at the Battle of Lade in 494 BC,
  it punished Miletus by selling all of the women and children into slavery,
  killing the men, and expelling all of the young men as eunuchs, "thereby
  assuring that no Miletus citizen would ever be born again". A year later the
  Athenians fined a playwright for putting the city's capture on stage.
standfirst: An ancient Greek city on the western coast of Anatolia, near the
  mouth of the Maeander in present-day Turkey. Renowned in antiquity for its
  wealth, maritime power and colonies, it was a centre of trade and innovation
  from the Bronze Age to the Roman period, and the home of Thales, Anaximander
  and Anaximenes.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: Balat, Didim, Aydın Province, Turkey
    from: "  location: Balat, Didim, Aydın Province, Turkey"
  - name: Region
    value: Aegean Region
    from: "  region: Aegean Region"
  - name: Built by
    value: Minoans (later Mycenaeans) and then Ionians (the later on a former
      Anatolian site)
    from: "    built-by: Minoans (later Mycenaeans) and then Ionians (the later on
      a former Anatolian site)"
  - name: Area of the site
    value: 90 ha (220 acres)
    from: |2-
        site-notes:
          area: 90 ha (220 acres)
          public-access: Yes
          website: Miletus Archaeological Site
  - name: Open to visitors
    value: Yes, as the Miletus Archaeological Site
    from: |2-
          public-access: Yes
          website: Miletus Archaeological Site
  - name: Its name in Greek
    value: Μίλητος
    from: "    native-label: Μίλητος"
pull-quotes:
  - text: Miletus was first founded and fortified above the sea by Cretans, where
      the Miletus of olden times is now situated, being settled by Sarpedon,
      who brought colonists from the Cretan Miletus and named the city after
      that Miletus
    attribution: Ephorus, quoted by Strabo
    section: minoan-period
  - text: he destroyed the trees and the crops of the land, and so returned to
      where he came from; for as the Milesians had command of the sea, it was
      of no use for his army to besiege their city
    attribution: Herodotus, describing the tactics of Alyattes against the
      Milesian countryside
    section: economy-and-land-use
  - text: Thales, the founder of this kind of philosophy, stated it to be water.
      (This is why he declared that the earth rests on water.)
    attribution: Aristotle
    section: philosophy
  - text: The things that are perish into the things from which they come to be,
      according to necessity, for they pay penalty and retribution to each
      other for their injustice in accordance with the ordering of time, as he
      says in rather poetical language.
    attribution: Aristotle, via Simplicius, on Anaximander
    section: philosophy
timeline:
  - when: 3500–3000 BC
    what: A Neolithic population inhabits the islands on which Miletus was
      originally placed.
    from: >-
      The earliest available archaeological evidence indicates that the
      islands on

      which Miletus was originally placed were inhabited by a [[ Neolithic ]]

      population in 3500–3000 BC.[[ ^4 ]] Pollen in [[ core samples |
      core-sample ]]
  - when: 2000 BC
    what: The earliest Minoan settlement of Miletus.
    from: The earliest Minoan settlement of Miletus dates to 2000 BC.[[ ^5 ]]
      Beginning
  - when: 1600 BC
    what: Wikidata places Miletus in Ionia, from 1600 BC to 1453.
    from: |2-
            - value: Ionia
              from: 1600 BC
              to: "1453"
  - when: c. 1450 to 1100 BC
    what: Miletus is a Mycenaean stronghold on the coast of Asia Minor.
    from: >-
      Miletus was a [[ Mycenaean | mycenae ]] stronghold on the coast of Asia
      Minor

      from c. 1450 to 1100 BC.[[ ^10 ]] In c. 1320 BC, the city supported an
  - when: c. 1320 BC
    what: The city backs Uhha-Ziti's anti-Hittite rebellion; Muršili's generals
      raid Millawanda and burn parts of it.
    from: >-
      anti-Hittite rebellion of [[ Uhha-Ziti ]] of nearby [[ Arzawa ]].

      [[ Muršili | muršili-ii ]] ordered his generals Mala-Ziti and [[ Gulla
      ]] to

      raid Millawanda, and they proceeded to burn parts of it; damage from

      [[ LHIIIA | helladic-period ]] found on-site has been associated with
      this

      raid.[[ ^11 ]] In addition the town was fortified according to a Hittite

      plan.[[ ^12 ]]
  - when: 11th century BC
    what: The inception recorded in Wikidata.
    from: "    inception: 11th century BC"
  - when: 494 BC
    what: Persia quashes the Ionian Revolt at the Battle of Lade and destroys the
      citizen body of Miletus.
    from: >-
      [[ Battle of Lade ]] in 494 BC and punished Miletus by selling all of
      the

      women and children into slavery, killing the men, and expelling all of
      the

      young men as eunuchs, thereby assuring that no Miletus citizen would
      ever be

      born again. A year afterward, [[ Phrynicus | phrynichus-tragic-poet ]]
  - when: 479 BC
    what: The Greeks defeat the Persians at Plataea and Miletus is freed from
      Persian rule.
    from: >-
      In 479 BC, the Greeks decisively defeated the Persians on the Greek
      mainland

      at the [[ Battle of Plataea ]], and Miletus was freed from Persian rule.
  - when: 387 BC
    what: The Peace of Antalcidas gives Artaxerxes II control of the Ionian
      city-states, Miletus among them.
    from: >-
      In 387 BC, the [[ Peace of Antalcidas ]] gave the Persian

      [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] under king [[ Artaxerxes II ]] control of the
      Greek

      city-states of [[ Ionia ]], including Miletus.
  - when: 334 BC
    what: Alexander's forces take the city in the Siege of Miletus; it reaches its
      greatest extent, about 90 hectares (220 acres).
    from: >-
      In 334 BC, the [[ Siege of Miletus ]] by the forces of

      [[ Alexander the Great ]] of Macedonia conquered the city. The conquest
      of

      most of the rest of Asia Minor soon followed. In this period, the city
      reached

      its greatest extent, occupying within its walls an area of approximately
      90

      hectares (220 acres).[[ ^24 ]]
  - when: 323 BC
    what: Alexander dies and Miletus passes to Ptolemy, governor of Caria, and his
      satrap of Lydia, Asander.
    from: >-
      When Alexander died in 323 BC, Miletus came under the control of
      Ptolemy,

      governor of [[ Caria ]], and his satrap of Lydia, [[ Asander ]], who had

      become autonomous.[[ ^25 ]] In 312 BC, Macedonian general
  - when: 280/279 BC
    what: The Milesians adopt a new chronological system based on the Seleucids.
    from: >-
      he was defeated by the Seleucids at the [[ Battle of Corupedium ]]. In
      280/279

      BC the Milesians adopted a new chronological system based on the
      Seleucids.
  - when: 279 BC
    what: Ptolemy II Philadelphus takes the city from Antiochus I; Egyptian sway
      lasts to the end of the century.
    from: >-
      In 279 BC, the city was taken from Seleucid king [[ Antiochus I ]] by
      Egyptian

      king [[ Ptolemy II Philadelphus ]], who donated a large area of land to
      cement

      their friendship, and it remained under Egyptian sway until the end of
      the

      century.[[ ^26 ]]
  - when: 133 BC
    what: After an alliance with Rome, Miletus becomes part of the province of
      Asia.
    from: >-
      After an alliance with Rome, in 133 BC the city became part of the
      province of

      Asia.
  - when: 57 AD
    what: The New Testament has the Apostle Paul meet the elders of the church of
      Ephesus at Miletus.
    from: >-
      The [[ New Testament ]] mentions Miletus as the site where the Apostle

      [[ Paul | paul-of-tarsus ]] in 57 AD met the elders of the

      [[ church | mother-church ]] of [[ Ephesus ]] near the close of his
      Third

      Missionary Journey, as recorded in [[ Acts of the Apostles ]] (Acts
      20:15–38).
  - when: "262"
    what: New city walls are built.
    from: In 262 new city walls were built.
  - when: "538"
    what: Justinian rebuilds the walls, but Miletus has become a small town.
    from: >-
      However the harbour was silting up and the economy was in decline. In
      538

      emperor [[ Justinian ]] rebuilt the walls but it had become a small
      town.
  - when: "1369"
    what: The archbishopric of Miletus is assumed by Stauropolis as the Anatolian
      Beyliks press in.
    from: >-
      headed by a [[ curator | curator-bonis ]].[[ ^27 ]][[ ^28 ]] In 1369,
      the

      archbishopric of Miletus, along with the one of

      [[ Antioch on the Meander | antioch-on-the-maeander ]], were assumed by

      [[ Stauropolis | stauropolis-diocese ]] due to their decline, as a
      result of

      the threat posed by the [[ Anatolian Beyliks ]].[[ ^29 ]]
  - when: 14th century
    what: Seljuk Turks conquer the city and use it as a port for trade with Venice.
    from: >-
      [[ Seljuk Turks ]] conquered the city in the 14th century and used
      Miletus as

      a port to trade with [[ Venice ]].
  - when: 15th century
    what: The Ottomans use Miletus as a harbour; as it silts up the city is
      abandoned.
    from: >-
      In the 15th century, the [[ Ottomans | ottoman-turks ]] utilized the
      city as a

      harbour during their rule in [[ Anatolia ]]. As the harbour became
      silted up,

      the city was abandoned. Due to ancient and subsequent
  - when: "1403"
    what: The Ilyas Bey Complex with its mosque is built.
    from: >-
      The [[ Ilyas Bey Complex | ilyas-bey-mosque ]] from 1403 with its mosque
      is a

      [[ Europa Nostra ]] awarded cultural heritage site in Miletus.
  - when: "1873"
    what: Olivier Rayet conducts the first excavations at Miletus.
    from: >-
      The first excavations in Miletus were conducted by the French
      archaeologist

      [[ Olivier Rayet ]] in 1873, followed by the German archaeologists
      Julius

      Hülsen and [[ Theodor Wiegand ]][[ ^31 ]][[ ^32 ]][[ ^33 ]] between 1899
      and

      1931.
image-plan:
  - file: The_Ionic_Stoa_of_Miletus.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The Ionic Stoa stood on the Sacred Way, the ceremonial road that ran
      from the city to the sanctuary of [[ Apollo ]] at [[ Didyma ]], roughly
      18 kilometers south.[[ ^60 ]]
  - file: Map_of_Lydia_ancient_times.jpg
    role: inline
    section: history
    caption: Miletus and other cities within the Lydian Empire. The city's
      independence survived a 12-year war fought against the [[ Lydian Empire
      ]].[[ ^18 ]]
  - file: Miletos_stadsplan_400.jpg
    role: full
    section: classical-greek-period
    caption: The plan of Milet in the Classical period, when the grid was carried
      across the whole area within the city wall. It became known as the
      "Hippodamian plan", after [[ Hippodamus of Miletus ]].[[ ^20 ]]
  - file: Rilievo_egizio_di_dea_madre,_da_santuario_di_atena_a_mileto,_VII_sec_ac.JPG
    role: inline
    section: egyptian-period
    caption: An Egyptian artefact found in Miletus.
  - file: Palation_Castle.jpg
    role: inline
    section: byzantine-period
    caption: Palation, the small Byzantine castle built on the hill beside the city.
  - file: İlyas_Bey.jpg
    role: inline
    section: turkish-rule
    caption: The Ottoman [[ Ilyas Bey mosque ]] on the Miletus site. The [[ Ilyas
      Bey Complex | ilyas-bey-mosque ]] of 1403 is a [[ Europa Nostra ]]
      awarded cultural heritage site.
  - file: Markttor_zu_Milet-Pergamonmuseum-2018.jpg
    role: full
    section: archaeological-excavations
    caption: The [[ Market Gate of Miletus ]], taken to Germany piece by piece
      after the first excavations of the 19th century and reassembled in the
      [[ Pergamon Museum ]] in [[ Berlin ]].
  - file: Miletus_Bay_silting_evolution_map-en.svg
    role: full
    section: geography
    caption: Miletus at the [[ Maeander River | büyük-menderes-river ]]'s mouth.
      Sedimentation of the harbor began at about 1000 BC, and by 300 AD [[
      Lake Bafa ]] had been created.[[ ^39 ]]
  - file: Colonies_of_Miletus.svg
    role: full
    section: colonies
    caption: Major Milesian colonial foundations around the Black Sea, phased by
      date. [[ Pliny the Elder ]] says Miletus founded over 90 colonies.
  - file: Milète_sanctuaire_d'Apollon.jpg
    role: inline
    section: apollo-delphinion
    caption: Sanctuary of Apollo. The Delphinion, sanctuary of Apollo Delphinios,
      originated in the 6th century BCE and remained in use through the Roman
      period.[[ ^62 ]]
  - file: Delphinion_Miletus_3D_Reconstruction.png
    role: inline
    section: apollo-delphinion
    caption: "A 3D reconstruction of the Delphinion: a rectangular peristyle
      courtyard lined with colonnades,[[ ^66 ]] the complex measuring
      approximately 60 × 50 m.[[ ^67 ]]"
  - file: Illustrerad_Verldshistoria_band_I_Ill_107.jpg
    role: inline
    section: notable-people
    caption: "[[ Thales of Miletus ]], mathematician, astronomer and pre-Socratic
      philosopher from the city, historically recognized as the first
      individual known to have entertained and engaged in scientific
      philosophy."
glossary:
  - term: Milesian school
    gloss: "Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes together: 6th-century BCE thinkers
      who investigated the material basis of the cosmos through rational,
      systematic inquiry rather than mythological narrative."
    from: >-
      Miletus played a foundational role in the origins of Western
      philosophical

      inquiry. In the 6th century BCE, thinkers such as [[ Thales ]],

      [[ Anaximander ]], and [[ Anaximenes | anaximenes-of-miletus
      ]]—collectively

      known as the [[ Milesian school ]]—began to investigate the material
      basis of

      the cosmos through rational, systematic inquiry rather than mythological

      narrative.[[ ^54 ]]
  - term: apeiron
    gloss: "Anaximander's term for the infinite or indefinite: the arkhē and
      element of things that are, neither water nor any of the other things
      called elements."
    from: >-
      Thales' student Anaximander introduced the concept of the [[ apeiron ]]
      (the

      infinite or indefinite) as a more abstract source of existence.
      According to

      Aristotle (via [[ Simplicius | simplicius-of-cilicia ]]):
      "Anaximander\...

      said that the apeiron was the arkhē and element of things that are, and
      he was

      the first to introduce this name for the arkhē. \...He says that the
      arkhē is

      neither water nor any of the other things called elements, but some
      other

      nature which is apeiron, out of which come to be all the heavens and the

      worlds in them. The things that are perish into the things from which
      they
  - term: Sacred Way
    gloss: The ceremonial road from Miletus to Didyma, a route for ritual
      processions and pilgrimage festivals.
    from: >-
      Miletus had several significant religious institutions, the most
      important of

      which was the sanctuary of [[ Apollo ]] at [[ Didyma ]], located roughly
      18

      kilometers south of the city. The sanctuary was connected to the city by
      a

      ceremonial road known as the Sacred Way, which served as a route for
      ritual

      processions and pilgrimage festivals.[[ ^60 ]]
  - term: Molpoi
    gloss: The guild responsible for musical and ceremonial functions at Miletus,
      whose duties were administered from the Delphinion.
    from: >-
      Ritual duties of the Molpoi, the guild responsible for musical and
      ceremonial

      functions, were administered from the site. Their responsibilities
      included

      offerings at designated stations along the Sacred Way, the ca. 18 km

      processional route leading from the Delphinion to the oracle of Apollo
      at

      Didyma.[[ ^64 ]] The sanctuary's shoreline placement, the presence of
      Kosmoi
  - term: Hippodamian plan
    gloss: The gridlike layout designed by Hippodamus of Miletus, which became the
      basic layout for new Hellenistic and Roman foundations.
    from: >-
      Archaic appearances.[[ ^23 ]] The city's gridlike layout was also
      constructed

      across all the area within the city wall, designed by

      [[ Hippodamus of Miletus ]]. It later became famous and was known as the

      "Hippodamian plan", serving as the basic layout for the new foundations
      of

      Hellenistic and [[ Roman | ancient-rome ]] cities.[[ ^20 ]]
  - term: stasis
    gloss: Internal conflict within a Greek city, one of the political factors
      that pushed Milesians overseas.
    from: >-
      easily available in [[ Ionia ]]. Political factors, such as

      [[ stasis | stasis-political-history ]] (internal conflict) and the
      impact of

      foreign powers like [[ Lydia ]] and [[ Persia | achaemenid-empire ]],
      also

      contributed, sometimes prompting groups or exiles to establish new
      settlements
  - term: Fikellura
    gloss: A pottery fabric named after a site on Rhodes to which it was once
      attributed; its production centre is now established as Miletus.
    from: >2-
          The name Fikellura derives from a site on the island of Rhodes to
      which this
          fabric has been attributed. It is now established that the center of
          production was Miletus.
  - term: mi-ra-ti-ja
    gloss: Mycenaean Greek for "women from Miletus", written in Linear B and used
      of female slaves at Pylos.
    from: >-
      In the last stage of LHIIIB, the citadel of Bronze Age [[ Pylos ]]
      counted

      among its female slaves a // mi-ra-ti-ja//, [[ Mycenaean Greek ]] for
      "women

      from Miletus", written in [[ Linear B ]] syllabic script.[[ ^14 ]]
further:
  - slug: achaemenid-empire
    why: "Persia ruled Miletus twice: after Cyrus defeated Croesus of Lydia, and
      again under the Peace of Antalcidas of 387 BC."
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: His forces took the city in the Siege of Miletus in 334 BC, after which
      it reached its greatest extent.
  - slug: ancient-greece
    why: Miletus founded dozens of colonies around the Mediterranean and Black
      Sea, significantly shaping the Greek world's expansion.
anchors:
  hook: >-
    When [[ Cyrus | cyrus-the-great ]] of Persia defeated [[ Croesus ]] of
    Lydia

    in the middle of the 6th century BC, Miletus fell under

    [[ Persian | achaemenid-empire ]] rule. In 499 BC, Miletus's [[ tyrant ]]

    [[ Aristagoras ]] became the leader of the [[ Ionian Revolt ]] against the

    Persians, who, under [[ Darius the Great ]], quashed this rebellion in the

    [[ Battle of Lade ]] in 494 BC and punished Miletus by selling all of the

    women and children into slavery, killing the men, and expelling all of the

    young men as eunuchs, thereby assuring that no Miletus citizen would ever
    be

    born again. A year afterward, [[ Phrynicus | phrynichus-tragic-poet ]]

    produced the tragedy //The Capture of Miletus// in Athens. The Athenians
    fined

    him for reminding them of their loss.[[ ^22 ]]
  standfirst: >-
    !!Miletus!! (Ancient Greek: Μίλητος,

    [[ romanised | romanization-of-ancient-greek ]]: //Mílētos//) was an

    influential ancient Greek city on the western coast of [[ Anatolia ]],
    near

    the mouth of the [[ Maeander River ]] in present day [[ Turkey ]].
    Renowned in

    antiquity for its wealth, maritime power, and extensive network of
    colonies,

    Miletus was a major center of trade, culture, and innovation from the

    [[ Bronze Age ]] through the [[ Roman period ]]. The city played a

    foundational role in the development of early [[ Greek philosophy ]] and

    science, serving as the home of the [[ Milesian school ]] with thinkers
    such

    as [[ Thales ]], [[ Anaximander ]], and

    [[ Anaximenes | anaximenes-of-miletus ]].
